WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
The Knee-OA Digital Twin Explorer: an interactive view into a digital twin of knee osteoarthritis, built from MRI.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.400
It shows what the model sees, and what the model does when one input is shifted. One 3D shape model for each tissue: femur, tibia, patella and the two menisci.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:14.300
Clicking a thumbnail switches the tissue in the main view; the thumbnails show the other tissues at the same setting.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:21.400
Three kinds of biomarker on the femur: cartilage T2, cartilage thickness and bone shape. Each is summarized by ten principal components.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:28.100
Moving the slider sweeps component 7 of femur cartilage T2 from −3 to +3 standard deviations. This is the pattern itself, drawn directly; no model is run.

6
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.200
The slider settles at +1 SD, the size of shift the paper's results are stated for.

7
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:38.400
The same sweep for cartilage thickness, component 7. Color is thickness in millimeters on the bone surface.

8
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:46.600
Medial meniscus shape, component 7. A shape component moves the surface itself; ±3 SD spans most of the variation seen in the cohort.

9
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.300
The Predict panel: femur cartilage T2 component 7 becomes the what-if. The shift snaps to −1, −0.5, 0, +0.5 or +1 SD; there are no in-between values.

10
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.800
The curve is cumulative risk over six model steps: the unshifted twin (reference) against the shifted one (counterfactual). The difference at the end updates with each shift.

11
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.700
Each scenario is the average over 150 held-out subjects, from one trained model.

12
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.400
The scrubber walks through the six steps. The shift is re-applied at every step, and the surface shows counterfactual minus reference.

13
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.900
Difference, geometry and color map: three views of the same step. These are model steps, not calendar time.

14
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.300
Back to Explore; the chosen what-if stays selected.

15
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.000
The second what-if available in Predict: femur cartilage thickness, component 2, with the same shifts and the same six steps.

16
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.400
The interface shows how the model behaves. It is not a clinical predictor.

17
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.100
The Population panel: the average shape of the stable-control and OA-incidence cohorts, as observed. This describes the data; it does not predict.

18
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.000
All three cohorts side by side. The difference panel uses one shared color scale, so differences are comparable.

19
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.600
Control minus the replacement cohort. Replacement reflects baseline severity, a different signal from incident OA, and is kept separate.
